@page "/maps"

<PageTitle>World maps · RioCharts for Blazor and .NET</PageTitle>

<h1>World maps</h1>
<p class="lead">
    A world map shows where something happens: countries colored by a value, bubbles at places within them, and a click on a country
    zooms into it. By default the map uses the Equal Earth projection, so countries keep their true relative size; the Mercator
    projection of web maps is one parameter away.
</p>

<Example Title="Countries and cities" Code="@SessionsCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            <code>MapSeries</code> colors each country by <code>YValue</code>; <code>XValue</code> is the country as an ISO 3166 code
            (<code>"SE"</code> or <code>"SWE"</code>) or its English name. The color gets stronger with the value's rank among the
            countries, so a few large values do not wash out the rest; set <code>Max</code> for a linear scale instead. <code>MapBubbleSeries</code> puts a bubble at each item's <code>Latitude</code> and
            <code>Longitude</code>, its area proportional to <code>YValue</code>. On the world map the places in a country add up to one
            bubble, so cities close together do not pile up; zoomed into a country, each place has its own (<code>GroupByCountry="false"</code>
            keeps them apart everywhere).
        </p>
        <p>
            A click zooms in one level: from the world to the country's continent, from the continent to the country. The path bar
            above (World › Europe › Sweden) leads back up. Bind <code>TreemapPath</code> to
            know, or set, the area shown, a continent such as <code>"Europe"</code> or a country code: here it is <strong>@(_region ?? "none, the whole world")</strong>.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="Place" Title="Active sessions" Height="420" @bind-TreemapPath="_region">
            <MapSeries Items="Countries" Name="Sessions" XValue="c => c.Name" YValue="c => c.Sessions" />
            <MapBubbleSeries Items="Cities" Name="Cities" XValue="c => c.Name" YValue="c => c.Sessions"
                             Latitude="c => c.Latitude" Longitude="c => c.Longitude" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Europe only" Code="@EuropeCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            Start <code>TreemapPath</code> at a continent to show only that part of the world: here <code>"Europe"</code>, which ends at the
            Urals. Countries outside it fade at the edges, so the map does not look cut off. A click on a country still zooms in, and
            the path bar leads out to the world and back. The other continents are <code>"Africa"</code>, <code>"Asia"</code>,
            <code>"North America"</code>, <code>"South America"</code> and <code>"Oceania"</code>; a country code zooms straight into
            one country.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="Place" Title="Active sessions in Europe" Height="420" @bind-TreemapPath="_europe">
            <MapSeries Items="Countries" Name="Sessions" XValue="c => c.Name" YValue="c => c.Sessions" />
            <MapBubbleSeries Items="Cities" Name="Cities" XValue="c => c.Name" YValue="c => c.Sessions"
                             Latitude="c => c.Latitude" Longitude="c => c.Longitude" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Bubbles only" Code="@BubblesCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            Without a <code>MapSeries</code>, the countries are a gray backdrop for the bubbles. <code>MarkerSize</code> sets the diameter
            of the largest bubble.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="Place" Height="300" Legend="NoLegend">
            <MapBubbleSeries Items="Cities" Name="Sessions" XValue="c => c.Name" YValue="c => c.Sessions"
                             Latitude="c => c.Latitude" Longitude="c => c.Longitude" MarkerSize="36" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Growth and decline" Code="@GrowthCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            <code>Midpoint</code> splits the colors at a value: countries above it take the series color, countries below it
            <code>BelowColor</code> (default <code>--rio-negative</code>). Each side is ranked on its own from the midpoint out, so the
            largest growth and the deepest decline both get the full color. The color scale shows the lowest value, the midpoint and the highest.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="Place" Title="Sessions against last year" Height="380">
            <MapSeries Items="Growth" Name="Change" XValue="c => c.Name" YValue="c => c.Sessions" Format="+0'%';-0'%';0'%'" Midpoint="0" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Projection" Code="@ProjectionCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            <code>Projection</code> on the chart sets how the round world is drawn flat. <code>MapProjection.EqualEarth</code>, the default,
            is equal-area: a country's size on the map compares fairly with any other. <code>MapProjection.Mercator</code> is the projection
            of web maps, the shapes most people know, but areas grow toward the poles (Greenland looks as large as Africa, which is
            fourteen times larger), so use it when the map should look familiar rather than when sizes matter. It is cut at 85° north
            and south, like web maps. In a JSON definition: <code>"projection": "mercator"</code>.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="Place" Height="420" Projection="_projection">
            <MapSeries Items="Countries" Name="Sessions" XValue="c => c.Name" YValue="c => c.Sessions" />
            <MapBubbleSeries Items="Cities" Name="Cities" XValue="c => c.Name" YValue="c => c.Sessions"
                             Latitude="c => c.Latitude" Longitude="c => c.Longitude" />
        </RioChart>
        <p class="note">
            @foreach (var projection in Enum.GetValues<MapProjection>())
            {
                <label><input type="radio" name="map-projection" checked="@(_projection == projection)" @onchange="() => _projection = projection" /> @projection</label>
            }
        </p>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<h2>Data</h2>
<p>
    The country outlines are Natural Earth's 1:110 million Admin 0 countries (public domain), built into <code>RioCharts.Core</code>.
    Very small countries, such as Singapore and Malta, are not in it at this scale. <code>WorldMap.Find</code> looks a country up by code
    or name.
</p>

